{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Popper's preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible without induction if rationality is redefined as the strategic selection of the hypothesis with the highest empirical content (boldness) that has survived severe tests, thereby maximizing the rate of potential error elimination rather than the probability of truth; however, this defense costs the scientist any assurance that the chosen theory is likely to be true or that nature will remain uniform, reducing scientific action to a non-probabilistic wager on boldness.","counterconditions":["If the agent requires a probabilistic guarantee of success before acting, the Popperian preference collapses into irrationality.","If 'severity' of a test implicitly relies on the assumption that nature is uniform (that past conditions predict future ones), the non-inductive claim is void.","If the goal of science is defined as 'finding the true theory' rather than 'eliminating error,' the preference for the boldest survivor is unjustified without induction."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The argument reconstructs Popper's distinction between 'logical probability' (which decreases with content) and 'corroboration' (a report of past test performance). It posits that preferring the better-corroborated theory is not an inductive inference about the future but a methodological rule to keep the most informative survivor in play. If Theory A has higher content and survived the same tests as Theory B, A is preferred because its falsification would yield more information. The mechanism fails if one assumes that 'preference for action' logically entails 'belief in probable success,' which Popper denies.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.65}],"simulation_proposals":[]}